<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  INSTALL PACKAGE IS android.car.usb.handler AGAIN, DELIBERATELY (owner request 2026-10-15: recent bench
  testing used that package name). package="zeno.gmccpa" below is the CLASS package / Gradle namespace
  and stays; tools/build_apk.sh renames the installed id at link time (aapt2 link rename-manifest-package,
  = Gradle applicationId). Every component name is fully qualified, so nothing below moves. Use
  android.car.usb.handler/zeno.gmccpa.<Class> in am commands (a short ".<Class>" would resolve against
  android.car.usb.handler and miss). The history below is kept as history: between 2026-09-08 and
  2026-10-15 the install package was zeno.gmccpa.

  It used to be android.car.usb.handler — a deliberate squat on the stock component GM points its
  config_UsbDeviceConnectionHandling_component at, on the theory that squatting the name would earn a
  silent per-UID USB grant with no dialog.

  IT DID NOT WORK (owner, 2026-09-10). This comment used to claim the squat "earned a silent per-UID
  USB grant on every attach with no dialog (device-proven 2026-08-17)" and was reverted only because
  the grant "landed about half the time". That overstates it in a way that could tempt someone to
  re-attempt the squat: THE APP STILL NEEDED THE USER TO GRANT PERMISSION FOR THE ADAPTER. The squat
  did not remove the permission requirement, which was the entire reason for doing it. Note the old
  wording was self-contradictory on its face — "on every attach, device-proven" and "about half the
  time in the field" cannot both be true; the owner's account resolves it.

  Reverted 2026-09-08. Impersonating a platform component is a large thing to carry for a benefit
  that never materialised.
  Corrected 2026-10-08 (plan v5.9 D6): the attach ACTIVITY route (UsbAttachActivity's intent-filter
  plus res/xml/device_filter.xml) never runs on this unit — GM routes attach to an absent fixed
  handler — and stays only as a dormant fallback. The live route is AdapterAttachReceiver below: the
  platform broadcasts USB_DEVICE_ATTACHED to the current user before that failed handler lookup, and
  the persistent USB grant ("always open", granted once) lets the auto-claim claim with no dialog.
  UsbBulkTransport polls hasPermission() rather than trusting the grant broadcast.

  Component android:name values stay FULLY QUALIFIED. They no longer have to be (a leading '.' would
  resolve correctly again), but they are unambiguous and it keeps the diff off every component line.
  (2026-09-08..10-15: adb/am commands took zeno.gmccpa. Since 2026-10-15 they take
  android.car.usb.handler — see README and docs/06.)
-->
<!-- NO versionCode/versionName here, deliberately (2026-09-11). `aapt2 link` honours its
     version-code/version-name flags only when the manifest declares NEITHER, so tools/build_apk.sh
     owns them: the code is the git commit count (monotonic on this single-branch repo) and the name
     is "4.0+<sha>". They were pinned at 7/"4.0" for the whole hardening run, which meant nothing on
     the device could say which build was installed and a stale APK installed `-r` over a newer one
     in silence. Re-adding them here does not override those flags, it disables them. -->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    package="zeno.gmccpa">

    <!-- Network + discovery: INTERNET only (a normal permission). The receiver, the connect-out and
         the self-hosted mDNS responder are raw sockets (ServerSocket / Socket / MulticastSocket), and
         the advert/browse is NsdManager; none of them needs more. The netprobe-era Wi-Fi, location
         and multicast-lock permissions and the cleartext-traffic flag were removed in v5.9 (D011):
         nothing in the app calls WifiManager, MulticastLock, ConnectivityManager, LocationManager
         or an HTTP stack. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET"/>
    <!-- Whole-OS log capture, the opt-in second scope of the in-app logger. READ_LOGS is
         signature|privileged|development: it can NEVER be obtained by declaring it, but the
         `development` flag means adb can grant it, and a permission can only be granted if the
         package DECLARES it — hence this line. One-time bench step, survives reboots and
         `install -r`, lost only on a full uninstall:
           adb shell pm grant android.car.usb.handler android.permission.READ_LOGS
         Without it the logger still captures this app's own process (no permission needed); with
         it, it also captures GM's CarPlay receiver, wpa_supplicant and the USB stack — the parts
         of the USB-grant and Bluetooth faults that happen before our process exists. -->
    <!-- Gear selection (VehiclePropertyIds.GEAR_SELECTION), the source for CarPlay's setLimitedUI.
         Measured `prot=normal` on gminfo38 2026-09-08 — install-time, no prompt, no privilege, which
         is the only reason an unprivileged app in /data/app can read gear at all. The cleaner
         CarDrivingStateManager needs CAR_DRIVING_STATE, which is signature|privileged and out of
         reach; CarUxRestrictions (no permission) is the fallback. See av/VehicleStateWatcher.kt. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.car.permission.CAR_POWERTRAIN"/>
    <!-- NIGHT_MODE (0x11200407) — the REAL day/night source on this head unit. Measured `prot=normal`
         2026-09-08. GM does not propagate vehicle night state into Android's uiMode here, so the
         Compose `isSystemInDarkTheme()` signal carlink_native uses reads day with night active. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.car.permission.CAR_EXTERIOR_ENVIRONMENT"/>
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.READ_LOGS"/>

    <!-- Lets capture start at boot rather than waiting for the first USB attach. Coverage does NOT
         depend on it (logcat's ring buffer is drained backwards on start, so an attach-time start
         still recovers what preceded it) — see BootReceiver's KDoc. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED"/>

    <!-- SYSTEM_ALERT_WINDOW (corrected 2026-10-08, plan v5.9 D6 R1): granted by `install -g` (its
         protection level includes `development`); the appop stays `default`, and default + granted IS
         effective. NEVER set the appop to ignore/deny — that overrides the grant. setting the appop to `allow` for
         user 10 is optional and lost on uninstall (docs/06 has the command; an XML comment cannot hold it). It is what admits the
         auto-claim's background FGS start (REASON 62) and the background launch of CarPlayActivity
         when the phone connects with the app closed. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.SYSTEM_ALERT_WINDOW"/>
    <!-- Keeps the process alive while a CarPlay session is up so backgrounding can't let LMK kill it. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE"/>
    <!-- Microphone uplink (Siri, phone calls). FOREGROUND_SERVICE_MICROPHONE is an API 34 permission:
         android-32 does not define it (javap on the SDK jar: absent), so on this unit it is INERT —
         an unknown string the installer ignores, kept only for a future targetSdk bump (corrected
         2026-10-08, D-DOC 3). What matters on API 32 is the service's foregroundServiceType="microphone"
         below. Note that on Android 11+ an FGS started from the BACKGROUND gets no while-in-use mic
         capability, so it captures silence until a VISIBLE start upgrades it — CarPlayActivity's own
         start of the service (plan v5.9 D6: an auto-claimed session gets the mic that way; U59-23). -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.RECORD_AUDIO"/>
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_MICROPHONE"/>

    <!-- required=false so the same APK still installs on a phone/tablet for bench work. -->

    <!-- targetSdk 30+ package-visibility filtering hides other apps' activities from resolveActivity,
         so without this the SAF probe in LogExport reports "no DocumentsUI" on a unit that has one and
         the export silently drops to the USB-volume rung. Declaring the intent we query for restores
         visibility of just that one. AAOS may genuinely lack DocumentsUI — the ladder handles that —
         but this makes the difference between "absent" and "invisible" a real answer. -->
    <queries>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.CREATE_DOCUMENT"/>
            <data android:mimeType="*/*"/>
        </intent>
    </queries>

    <uses-feature android:name="android.hardware.type.automotive" android:required="false"/>
    <!-- USB host mode: how the app claims the CCPA adapter at 0x1314:0x2d00 and speaks OCBM. -->
    <uses-feature android:name="android.hardware.usb.host" android:required="false"/>

    <!-- allowBackup is OFF, and set explicitly because the default is true and Lint's AllowBackup
         check is silent at targetSdk >= 31, so nothing would ever have flagged the omission.
         filesDir/logs/ holds whole-OS logcat captures — wpa_supplicant and Bluetooth lines included
         once READ_LOGS is granted — and that must not be eligible for cloud backup.
         What this attribute does NOT cover is device-to-device migration: on API 32 (the only
         platform this app ships to — the gminfo3.7 unit, permanently) BackupEligibilityRules ignores
         allowBackup for OperationType.MIGRATION, so a D2D transfer would still copy filesDir.
         Excluding that needs android:dataExtractionRules with a <device-transfer> exclusion, and the
         owner's decision is that a second rules surface is more than the threat warrants on a head
         unit that is never the source of a phone-style migration. Not an oversight; do not add it
         without re-opening that decision. -->
    <!-- icon: outline USB trident in the HomeScreen's own grey wireframe style. Without it the tile
         showed the stock Android robot. It is a PLAIN vector on purpose: an <adaptive-icon> renders a
         transparent background as a black disc (see the comment in res/drawable/ic_launcher_usb.xml).
         No roundIcon, for the same reason. -->
    <application
        android:label="GM CCPA"
        android:icon="@drawable/ic_launcher_usb"
        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:theme="@android:style/Theme.DeviceDefault">

        <!-- Present so the class loader resolves android.car at runtime on the head unit; the prober
             reaches CarUxRestrictions reflectively, so the build needs no android.car stub jar. -->
        <uses-library android:name="android.car" android:required="false"/>

        <!-- NoActionBar: the DeviceDefault action bar rendered the label in a washed-out strip across
             the top and stole vertical space the state readout needs. The screen paints its own
             background and owns the whole area. -->
        <!-- The attach ACTIVITY route: a NoDisplay trampoline that filters to the OCBM adapter
             (0x1314:0x2d00) via device_filter and forwards into MainActivity's attach path. DORMANT on
             this unit (corrected 2026-10-08, plan v5.9 D6): GM routes attach to an absent fixed
             handler, so the resolver never launches it; AdapterAttachReceiver is the live route.
             Kept as the fallback for a unit whose resolver does run. Before 2026-09-08 this was the
             fixed-handler squat (android.car.usb.handler.UsbHostManagementActivity); since 2026-10-15
             that component exists again as the activity-alias right below. -->
        <activity
            android:name="zeno.gmccpa.UsbAttachActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:launchMode="singleInstance"
            android:taskAffinity=""
            android:excludeFromRecents="true"
            android:noHistory="true"
            android:theme="@android:style/Theme.NoDisplay">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.hardware.usb.action.USB_DEVICE_ATTACHED"/>
            </intent-filter>
            <meta-data android:name="android.hardware.usb.action.USB_DEVICE_ATTACHED"
                android:resource="@xml/device_filter"/>
        </activity>
        <!-- THE FIXED-HANDLER COMPONENT (2026-10-15, with the android.car.usb.handler install package).
             GM's config_UsbDeviceConnectionHandling_component is the literal
             android.car.usb.handler/android.car.usb.handler.UsbHostManagementActivity. On every USB
             host attach the framework (deviceAttachedForFixedHandler) broadcasts, grants device
             permission to this package's uid, then starts THAT component explicitly. Same component
             name as the pre-2026-09-08 squat class, but as an alias onto UsbAttachActivity so no class
             leaves zeno.gmccpa. The grant itself needs only the package; the alias is what gives a
             post-grant entry: AdapterAttachReceiver's broadcast is sent BEFORE the grant, so its
             background pre-check can read "no permission" and SKIP_NO_GRANT, while this start comes
             after it on the same system_server thread. It fires for EVERY USB device; the trampoline
             ignores anything that is not 0x1314:0x2d00. No intent-filter: one plug must not resolve
             to two activities of this app on a unit whose ordinary resolver runs. -->
        <activity-alias
            android:name="android.car.usb.handler.UsbHostManagementActivity"
            android:targetActivity="zeno.gmccpa.UsbAttachActivity"
            android:exported="true"/>

        <activity
            android:name="zeno.gmccpa.MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:launchMode="singleTask"
            android:theme="@android:style/Theme.DeviceDefault.NoActionBar"
            android:configChanges="orientation|screenSize|keyboardHidden|screenLayout|uiMode">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN"/>
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER"/>
            </intent-filter>
            <!-- The AAOS driver-distraction gate: activities marked distractionOptimized are eligible
                 to show while the vehicle is in motion. Combined with a Play-attributed install
                 (adb install -i com.android.vending) this is what lets it run moving. -->
            <meta-data android:name="distractionOptimized" android:value="true"/>
            <!-- No attach filter here: the (dormant) UsbAttachActivity forwards a matching
                 ACTION_USB_DEVICE_ATTACHED intent; the live attach route is AdapterAttachReceiver,
                 which claims with no UI at all (plan v5.9 D6). -->
        </activity>
        <activity
            android:name="zeno.gmccpa.av.CarPlayActivity"
            android:exported="false"
            android:launchMode="singleTop"
            android:taskAffinity=".carplay"
            android:screenOrientation="landscape"
            android:configChanges="orientation|screenSize|keyboardHidden|screenLayout|uiMode"
            android:theme="@android:style/Theme.NoTitleBar.Fullscreen">
            <!-- Without this, AAOS UX restrictions block this Activity the moment the truck moves —
                 which is precisely when a CarPlay screen must stay up. MainActivity already carries it. -->
            <meta-data android:name="distractionOptimized" android:value="true" />
        </activity>

        <!-- Foreground service that pins the process at foreground priority for the session lifetime
             (C3). API 32: no foregroundServiceType attribute is mandated. -->
        <!-- foregroundServiceType="microphone" is required for the mic uplink (the
             FOREGROUND_SERVICE_MICROPHONE permission above is API 34 and inert on this unit either
             way): from Android 11 an FGS needs the
             microphone TYPE to capture, and without it AudioRecord.read() silently returns the
             full requested byte count filled with ZEROS the moment the Activity stops being
             visible — exactly the GM-dialog / reverse-camera / app-switch case this service
             exists for. Siri would hear silence, with no error anywhere. -->
        <service
            android:name="zeno.gmccpa.av.CarPlaySessionService"
            android:exported="false"
            android:foregroundServiceType="microphone" />

        <!-- AAOS media source registration.
             AAOS builds its source switcher by querying for services answering
             android.media.browse.MediaBrowserService. Without this the app plays audio fine but is
             invisible to the switcher, so selecting FM leaves no way back to CarPlay except
             relaunching by hand. MUST be exported: the querying process is the media host
             (CarMediaService / the GM media apps), not us. -->
        <service
            android:name="zeno.gmccpa.av.CarPlayMediaBrowserService"
            android:exported="true">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.media.browse.MediaBrowserService" />
            </intent-filter>
            <!-- Kept, effect on gminfo37 UNKNOWN. The isMediaTemplate() / "homescreen Media card"
                 reasoning that used to stand here is AOSP CarLauncher behaviour; this head unit's HOME
                 is GM HomeScreen (no CarLauncher installed); a decompile of HomeScreen showed no
                 reference to this key (one trace, 2026-10-08). A GM media picker may read it (the CT5
                 GMCarMediaService dex carries the string), so it stays. Removing it does NOT remove
                 the second app-grid tile; the key below does. -->
            <meta-data android:name="androidx.car.app.launchable" android:value="true" />
            <!-- One launcher icon (owner request 2026-10-08). GM HomeScreen adds an app-grid tile for
                 EVERY MediaBrowserService, beside the LAUNCHER activity; tapping it opened the GM media
                 app (MEDIA_TEMPLATE), not ours. HIDDEN keeps that tile off the grid while the service
                 stays bound and selectable as a media source, as carlink_native does. SERVICE level
                 only: HomeScreen merges application metaData into every component, so the key at
                 application level would also hide MainActivity. Whether GM's media picker apps read it
                 is UNKNOWN (bench: GM Audio must still select gm_ccpa). -->
            <meta-data android:name="com.gm.homescreen.visibility" android:value="HIDDEN" />
        </service>

        <!-- Declares the app to AAOS as a media app; see res/xml/automotive_app_desc.xml. -->
        <meta-data
            android:name="com.android.automotive"
            android:resource="@xml/automotive_app_desc" />
        <!-- Fully qualified like every other component here — NECESSARY again since 2026-10-15: the
             applicationId is android.car.usb.handler, so a leading '.' would resolve against it. -->
        <!-- plan v5.9 D6 trigger T1: the adapter attached (hot plug, box reboot) with the app closed.
             exported=false: the sender is system_server (its uid passes any receiver check); a shell
             or app broadcast is refused. One action, no meta-data (device filters are matched for
             activities only); the receiver checks 0x1314:0x2d00 itself. -->
        <receiver
            android:name="zeno.gmccpa.AdapterAttachReceiver"
            android:exported="false">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.hardware.usb.action.USB_DEVICE_ATTACHED"/>
            </intent-filter>
        </receiver>
        <!-- BOOT_COMPLETED: capture start, and D6 trigger T2 (the boot auto-claim). -->
        <receiver
            android:name="zeno.gmccpa.logging.BootReceiver"
            android:exported="true"
            android:directBootAware="false">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.BOOT_COMPLETED"/>
            </intent-filter>
        </receiver>

    </application>
</manifest>
